[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"AWS S3: Buckets, Objects, Versioning, and Lifecycle Policies"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (S3) is a highly scalable, durable, and secure object storage service. This tutorial covers the core concepts of S3, including buckets, objects, versioning, and lifecycle policies."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Buckets"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A bucket is a container for objects stored in S3. Every object is stored in a bucket. Buckets have globally unique names, meaning no two AWS accounts can have a bucket with the same name. When creating a bucket, you must also choose an AWS Region. The region affects latency, costs, and regulatory requirements."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bucket names must be unique across all of AWS."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bucket names must adhere to DNS naming conventions."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Buckets are region-specific."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Objects"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Objects are the fundamental entities stored in S3. Each object consists of data and metadata. The data is the actual content you are storing (e.g., images, videos, documents). Metadata is data about the data, such as content type, size, and last modified date."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Objects are stored in buckets."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Each object has a key, which is its unique identifier within the bucket."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Objects can have metadata associated with them."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Versioning"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Versioning allows you to keep multiple versions of an object in the same bucket. When versioning is enabled, every PUT, POST, COPY, or DELETE operation creates a new version of the object. This protects you from accidental overwrites or deletions."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Versioning is disabled by default."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Once enabled, versioning cannot be disabled, only suspended."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Each version of an object has a unique version ID."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Lifecycle Policies"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lifecycle policies automate the process of moving objects to different storage classes or deleting them after a specified period. This helps you optimize storage costs by moving infrequently accessed data to cheaper storage classes like S3 Glacier or S3 Intelligent-Tiering, or by automatically deleting old data that is no longer needed."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lifecycle policies can be applied to entire buckets or specific prefixes."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"You can define rules to transition objects to different storage classes (e.g., Standard to Intelligent-Tiering to Glacier)."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"You can define rules to expire objects after a certain number of days."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"Example Lifecycle Policy (JSON):
{
  "Rules": [
    {
      "ID": "MoveToGlacier",
      "Prefix": "logs/",
      "Status": "Enabled",
      "Transitions": [
        {
          "Date": "2024-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
          "StorageClass": "GLACIER"
        }
      ],
      "Expiration": {
        "Days": 365
      }
    }
  ]
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example lifecycle policy moves all objects with the prefix 'logs/' to Glacier storage on January 1, 2024, and expires them after 365 days."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"S3 provides a robust and versatile storage solution for a wide range of use cases. Understanding buckets, objects, versioning, and lifecycle policies is crucial for effectively managing your data and optimizing costs within AWS."}]
